Stage 31 — Common Kotlin Investigation Mistakes

Avoid

Treating generated continuation classes as handwritten logic.

Misinterpreting default-argument dispatcher methods.

Ignoring Kotlin metadata.

Confusing extension functions with instance methods.

Assuming decompiled Java accurately represents Kotlin source.

Overlooking delegated properties and compiler-generated accessors.

Following coroutine state labels instead of reconstructing the original linear flow.



---

Stage 32 — Kotlin APK Methodology Master Checklist

By the end of this stage, you should be able to:

✅ Identify Kotlin-specific compiler artifacts and metadata.

✅ Recover original Kotlin language constructs from DEX, Smali, or decompiled output.

✅ Distinguish handwritten application logic from compiler-generated infrastructure.

✅ Reconstruct coroutine execution, Flow pipelines, and asynchronous state machines.

✅ Recover properties, delegated properties, extension functions, data classes, sealed hierarchies, and object declarations.

✅ Analyze mixed Java/Kotlin applications while preserving architectural intent.

✅ Correlate static analysis with runtime behavior to validate reconstructed semantics.

✅ Produce accurate documentation explaining both the application's architecture and the Kotlin compiler transformations that shaped its implementation.



